<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  robird (X5.15): a robotic peregrine falcon after van der Grinten et al. (2015) flying a horizontal 1:2 figure of eight.
  Generated by design/build_model.py. Z-up, metres. The link "fuselage" is the body frame at the centre of mass (x forward, y left,
  z up); its pose is bound to the seven pose signals. Per wing: "flap" (hub link, about the forward axis), "torsion" (about the
  span axis, the aeroelastic twist of the root joint, bound to its deflection), "bend" (outer against inner segment). The tail has two
  elevons (about the span axis, positive = leading edge up) and a rudder (about the vertical axis).
  The bird is drawn 20 times larger than it is (22.0 m wing span on screen for 1.10 m)
  because the figure is 220 m wide and the viewer camera does not follow; all joint offsets are scaled the same way, the pose
  binding is in true metres. The orange spheres mark the Lissajous path.
-->
